{
    "openapi": "3.1.0",
    "info": {
        "title": "SchoolTracs Public API",
        "version": "v1",
        "summary": "Read and write your centre's students, lessons, enrolments and billing from your own software.",
        "description": "The Public API lets your own software talk to SchoolTracs directly: pull tomorrow's\ntimetable into a display screen, push a web enquiry form straight into your leads, mark\nattendance from a tablet app, or sync invoices into your accounting system.\n\nEverything is JSON over HTTPS. There are no SDKs to install — an API key and an HTTP\nclient are enough.\n\n## Getting started\n\n1. In SchoolTracs, open **Settings → Integration → API access** and create a key. You can\n   hold up to 5 live keys at a time.\n2. Send that key as an `X-Api-Key` header on every request.\n3. Start with `GET /v1/centers` — it is the cheapest call that proves your key works and\n   tells you which centre IDs you are allowed to touch.\n\n```bash\ncurl https://web.schooltracs.com/v4/api/v1/centers \\\n  -H \"X-Api-Key: YOUR_KEY_HERE\"\n```\n\n## How a key decides what you can see\n\nA key is bound to one organisation and one centre when it is created, and that binding\ncannot be overridden by anything you put in a request. Every query is silently narrowed to\nthe centres your key covers. Asking for a record that belongs to someone else returns\n**404**, not 403 — the API will not confirm that a record exists outside your own data.\n\nYou can omit `centerId`, including when creating records; it defaults to your API key's\ncentre. If you supply `centerId`, it must match that centre.\n\n## What comes back\n\nA single record:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"data\": { \"id\": 9101, \"name\": \"Amelia Tsang\" },\n  \"meta\": { \"message\": \"OK\", \"apiVersion\": \"v1\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-03-14T09:20:11+00:00\" }\n}\n```\n\nA list adds paging information to `meta` — and note that a list response carries **no**\n`message` key, only single-record responses do:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"data\": [ { \"id\": 9101, \"name\": \"Amelia Tsang\" } ],\n  \"meta\": {\n    \"currentPage\": 1, \"from\": 1, \"lastPage\": 6, \"perPage\": 50, \"to\": 50, \"total\": 284,\n    \"links\": {\n      \"first\": \"https://web.schooltracs.com/v4/api/v1/customers?page=1\",\n      \"last\":  \"https://web.schooltracs.com/v4/api/v1/customers?page=6\",\n      \"prev\":  null,\n      \"next\":  \"https://web.schooltracs.com/v4/api/v1/customers?page=2\"\n    },\n    \"apiVersion\": \"v1\",\n    \"timestamp\": \"2026-03-14T09:20:11+00:00\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe simplest way to walk a whole list is to follow `meta.links.next` until it is `null`,\nrather than counting pages yourself. `lastPage` and the `last`/`next` links respect your\nplan's page-depth limit. `total` counts all matching records, including those beyond the\npages your plan allows.\n\n## Dates and times\n\nCalendar dates use `YYYY-MM-DD`, for example `2026-03-17`, with no time or timezone.\nUse this format for date filters too, including filters on timestamp fields such as\n`filter[created][gte]=2026-03-17`: those filters compare calendar dates.\n\nResponse timestamps such as `created`, `updated`, `attendanceUpdated`, `postedDate` and\n`meta.timestamp` use ISO 8601: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss±HH:mm`, for example\n`2026-03-17T08:31:02+00:00`. The offset identifies the timezone; `+00:00` means UTC.\nUse the supplied offset when converting a timestamp to your local time.\n\nLesson `startTime` and `endTime` are timetable times without a timezone offset, paired\nwith the lesson's `date`. They use `HH:mm` or `HH:mm:ss`, for example `16:30` or\n`16:30:00` (4:30 pm). Both forms are accepted when creating or updating a lesson;\nresponses return the stored value. For a lesson ending after midnight, `endTime` can\nhave an hour of 24 or more: `25:30:00` means 1:30 am on the day after `date`.\nWhen creating a lesson, the latest permitted end time is `32:00` or `32:00:00`\n(8:00 am on the next day); any later value is rejected.\nMinutes and seconds must be `00`–`59`, and `startTime` hours must be `00`–`23`.\nMalformed time components such as `31:60` are rejected with `422` on create and update.\n\nThe webhook `SchoolTracs-Timestamp` header uses a different representation: an integer\nnumber of seconds since `1970-01-01T00:00:00Z` (Unix time), not milliseconds.\n\n## Shaping a query\n\nEvery list endpoint accepts the same four tools. The exact field names each one accepts\nare listed on the endpoint itself.\n\n| Tool | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `filter[field]=value` | Narrow the result set. Operators like `[gte]`, `[in]` and `[exists]` are described per field. |\n| `sort=-created,name` | Order the results. A leading `-` sorts descending; list several fields comma separated. |\n| `fields[table]=id,name` | Return only the fields you need. `id` always comes back. The key is the model's table name, so it is `fields[customer]`, `fields[activity]`, `fields[order_item]`. |\n| `include=customer,receipts` | Embed related records instead of making a second call. |\n\nThree filter behaviours are worth knowing up front, because they are not guessable:\n\n- **`exists` asks about null, not about truth.** `filter[email][exists]=true` means \"has an\n  email address on file\"; `false` means the field is empty. It accepts `1/true/yes/on` and\n  `0/false/no/off/null`.\n- **JSON values are accepted anywhere a bracket would be.** `filter[created]={\"gte\":\"2026-01-01\"}`\n  does the same thing as `filter[created][gte]=2026-01-01`. Useful when your HTTP client\n  makes nested query keys awkward.\n- **Exact-match fields can reach through a relation.** `filter[customer.email]=…` works on\n  endpoints whose `customer` is an exact-match field, and an explicit `null` means \"is unset\".\n\n## Deleted, refunded and reversed records\n\nBy default you see live records only. `?withTrashed=true` adds soft-deleted ones and\n`?onlyTrashed=true` returns just those — both on the PAID plan only; a FREE key gets the\nordinary result set back instead of an error.\n\nFor money records, \"deleted\" is not the same thing as \"undone\":\n\n- an **order item** is refunded by reducing its own quantity and total, so the row is still\n  there — use `filter[refunded]=true` to see those;\n- a **receipt** is reversed by a later receipt pointing back at it, and the original row is\n  never deleted — use `filter[reversed]=true`.\n\n## FREE and PAID plans\n\nYour plan is a property of the centre, not of the key. It changes the limits, and on some\nresources it changes what comes back at all.\n\n| Limit | FREE | PAID |\n|---|---|---|\n| Live API keys | 5 | 5 |\n| Requests per minute | 20 | 120 |\n| Requests per day | 500 | 5,000 |\n| Requests per month | 2,500 | 150,000 |\n| Write requests per minute | no separate budget — writes come out of the overall limit | 60 |\n| Requests per minute across all your keys | — | 1,200 |\n| Requests in flight at once | 1 | 5 |\n| Largest page | 50 | 500 |\n| Pages you can reach | 5 | 200 |\n| Scopes | `read:*`, `write:customers`, `write:leads` | `read:*`, `write:*`, `webhook:*` |\n\nOn top of those, **historical pulls are capped at 5 per minute on every plan**. A request\ncounts as a historical pull when you page past page 3, or when a date filter is open-ended\nor spans more than 90 days. If you are backfilling, pace it.\n\nWhat a FREE key cannot do:\n\n- **Write most things.** A FREE key can write `customers` and `leads` — enough to push\n  registrations and web enquiries in — but nothing else. Every other POST and PATCH is\n  rejected with `403` before it reaches a controller.\n- **Read invoices, receipts, order items or journal entries.** Those four resources refuse\n  a FREE key outright.\n- **See the full customer record.** `GET /v1/customers` returns 16 fields on FREE instead of\n  the full set — the extra ones are simply absent rather than null. Each PAID-only field is\n  marked in the schema below.\n- **Look far back.** Lessons are limited to a rolling window of today ±6 days, and\n  enrolments to currently-active ones. On PAID those become real date-range queries, still\n  bounded: at most 3 years back and 1 year wide per request.\n\n## Webhooks\n\nFour resources can push a change to your own server the moment it happens, instead of you\npolling for it: **students, orders, invoices and receipts**. Nothing else in this API fires a\nwebhook — lessons, courses, fees, staff and journal notes don't, however you change them.\n\n### Setting one up\n\nThere's no REST endpoint for this. Subscribe from **Settings → Integration → API access**, in\nthe **Webhook** section: pick a `https://` delivery URL and turn on the entity-level events you\nwant (`student.*`, `order.*`, `invoice.*`, `receipt.*`). SchoolTracs generates the signing\nsecret when you save the subscription and shows it to you there, so you can have your\nverification in place before the first delivery arrives. Webhooks need the `webhook:*` scope,\nwhich only exists on the PAID plan — the option is unavailable on FREE.\n\nDeliveries fire for a change made **anywhere** in SchoolTracs — the staff app, checkout, an\nimport — not only for writes made through this API. An enrolment write here, for instance,\ncreates or updates the underlying course order and can trigger an `order.*` delivery even\nthough there is no direct \"orders\" write endpoint in this API.\n\n### What you receive\n\nA `POST` to your delivery URL, with a body shaped like this:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"3f9d2e0a-1b2c-4d5e-8f6a-7b8c9d0e1f23\",\n  \"event\": \"invoice.paid\",\n  \"apiVersion\": \"v1\",\n  \"created\": \"2026-03-18T01:05:11+00:00\",\n  \"data\": { \"object\": { \"...\": \"the same shape GET /v1/invoices/{id} returns\" } }\n}\n```\n\n`data.object` is the resource **re-read at delivery time**, not a diff of what changed or a\nsnapshot of the moment the event fired. One behaviour worth knowing before you rely on it: it\nis built without any `?include=`, so a field that would normally only appear when you ask for\nit — `customer`, `center`, `receipt`, and the like — comes back as an **empty object `{}`**\nrather than being left out of the payload the way a REST response leaves it out. Treat `{}` as\n\"not sent\", not as \"this relation is empty\" — re-fetch the record over REST if you need it.\n\n### Events\n\n| Entity | Events |\n|---|---|\n| `student` | `created`, `updated`, `deleted` |\n| `order` | `created`, `updated`, `deleted` |\n| `invoice` | `issued` (created), `updated`, `voided` (deleted), `paid` |\n| `receipt` | `created`, `updated`, `deleted` |\n\n`invoice.paid` fires the moment `paid` first reaches `total` — not on every save of an\nalready-settled invoice, and not on a partial payment that doesn't clear the balance. It can\narrive **alongside** `invoice.updated` for the same write, if that write both changes something\nelse and clears the balance — don't assume one write means one delivery.\n\nSubscribing to an entity's `*` wildcard (`invoice.*`, and so on) covers every event for that\nentity in one subscription, rather than wiring each one up separately.\n\n### Verifying a delivery\n\nEvery request carries:\n\n| Header | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `SchoolTracs-Event` | The event name, e.g. `invoice.paid`. |\n| `SchoolTracs-Delivery` | A UUID unique to this delivery — use it to de-duplicate retries. |\n| `SchoolTracs-Timestamp` | Unix timestamp, in seconds, matching what was signed. |\n| `X-Signature` | `v1=<hex-encoded HMAC>` — `HMAC-SHA256(\"{timestamp}.{raw body}\", your signing secret)`. Recompute it and compare before trusting a payload. |\n\nYour delivery URL must be `https://` and publicly resolvable — `localhost`, `.local` hostnames,\nand private or reserved IP ranges are all rejected when you save the subscription.\n\n### Retries and debouncing\n\nA non-2xx response, or a network failure, counts as a failed delivery: it is retried roughly\nevery 5 minutes for up to 24 hours, then given up on. **Every** attempt — successful or not —\nis logged and visible under **Settings → Logs → Webhook**, including the request that was sent\nand the response or error that came back.\n\nSeveral saves to the same record within a 5-second window collapse into a single delivery of\nthe final state, rather than one per save — so don't assume one API write produces one webhook\ncall.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\nControllers return this shape:\n\n```json\n{ \"status\": \"error\", \"message\": \"Validation failed.\", \"errors\": { \"name\": [\"The name field is required.\"] } }\n```\n\nAuth and rate limiting run *before* any controller, so those failures are shorter — just\n`{\"message\": \"…\"}`, with no `status` or `errors` key. Handle both.\n\n| Status | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| `400` | The request was understood but the operation is not allowed right now — the message explains. |\n| `401` | Missing, unknown or revoked `X-Api-Key`. |\n| `403` | Your plan does not include this endpoint, or the record belongs to a centre your key cannot write to. |\n| `404` | No such record — including records that exist but belong to another centre. |\n| `409` | A sales action wants confirmation first. Re-send with `confirmed`. |\n| `422` | Validation failed, or you exceeded a plan limit on page size, page depth or date range. |\n| `429` | A rate limit ran out. `window` says which one and `retry_after` says for how long. |\n| `500` | Something broke on our side. The message is deliberately generic; the details are in our logs. |\n\nTwo limits return `422` rather than quietly doing something else: asking for a page size\nabove your plan's ceiling, and paging deeper than your plan allows. Neither is clamped.\n\n## There is no delete endpoint\n\nNothing in this API deletes a record. There is no `DELETE` verb on any resource, by design,\nand no write scope grants deletion — not even `write:*`. The nearest equivalents are two\nexplicit, named actions that do the right bookkeeping either side of the record they close\nout: `POST /v1/enrollments/{id}/cancel` and `POST /v1/invoices/{id}/void`.\n\nTrying to reach deletion through a field does not work either. Sending `deleted` or\n`deletedAt` as a truthy value in the body of **any** write is rejected outright with a\n`403` before the request reaches the endpoint — it is not quietly dropped, and it will not\ntake a record down.\n\nInvoices are also not creatable. They are produced as a side effect of ordering and\ncheckout, so the API exposes only a due-date change and a void."
    },
    "servers": [
        {
            "url": "https://web.schooltracs.com/v4/api/v1",
            "description": "The Public API v1 endpoint. The same host for every organisation — your API key determines which organisation and centre you can access."
        }
    ],
    "security": [
        {
            "ApiKeyAuth": []
        }
    ],
    "tags": [
        {
            "name": "Lessons",
            "description": "A lesson is one dated, timed session of a course — the thing that appears on a timetable. Attendance and make-up status live on each lesson, per student."
        },
        {
            "name": "Leads",
            "description": "Enquiries that have not become students yet — the people who filled in a form, walked in, or rang up. A lead carries the same contact details as a student, plus where it came from and how far along it is. Reading and writing leads both work on every plan."
        },
        {
            "name": "Centres",
            "description": "The branches your key can see. Read-only — centres are set up in SchoolTracs itself."
        },
        {
            "name": "Courses",
            "description": "A course is the template a timetable is generated from: what it is called, how long each lesson runs, how many students fit, and when it runs between."
        },
        {
            "name": "Students",
            "description": "Students — called customers throughout the API because they are also who invoices are addressed to. This is the resource where the FREE plan visibly returns less: 16 fields instead of the full record."
        },
        {
            "name": "Enrolments",
            "description": "A student's ongoing place on a course, together with what it costs and how much of it has been paid or scheduled. Behind the scenes an enrolment **is** the course order, which is why `id` and `orderId` are always the same number — you can read either one off a response and hand it back as `orderId` on a later call."
        },
        {
            "name": "Rooms",
            "description": "Rooms and other bookable resources that lessons are scheduled into."
        },
        {
            "name": "Fees",
            "description": "What a course costs and how it is sold — per lesson, per month, per term, or as a block of quota. A fee is what an enrolment is priced from, so you need its ID before you can book anyone."
        },
        {
            "name": "Invoices",
            "description": "What a student has been billed. Invoices are produced by ordering and checkout rather than created directly, so this API exposes only two write actions on them: moving the due date, and voiding. **PAID plan only.**"
        },
        {
            "name": "Journal",
            "description": "Progress notes staff write about a student — lesson feedback, behaviour notes, things to follow up. Notes can be private to staff or shared with the student. **PAID plan only.**"
        },
        {
            "name": "Orders",
            "description": "Every individual thing a student has been sold — a course enrolment, a book, a registration fee — with its price, discount, tax and payment state. This is the finest-grained view of sales. **PAID plan only.**"
        },
        {
            "name": "Products",
            "description": "Physical or one-off items you sell alongside tuition — books, uniforms, exam entries — with stock and cost."
        },
        {
            "name": "Receipts",
            "description": "Money actually received, and what it was applied to. **PAID plan only.**"
        },
        {
            "name": "Staff",
            "description": "Teachers and administrators — who they are, how to reach them, and which calendar their lessons publish to."
        }
    ],
    "paths": {
        "/activities": {
            "get": {
                "operationId": "getActivities",
                "tags": [
                    "Lessons"
                ],
                "summary": "List lessons",
                "description": "**Why you'll use it**\n\nThis is the timetable endpoint. Use it to build a wall display, feed a teacher's app, drive attendance taking, or reconcile who was booked where.\n\n**Before you send**\n\nHow far you can see depends on your plan, and this is the one place where a FREE key changes the results rather than refusing:\n\n- **FREE** always returns a rolling window of today −6 to +6 days. A `filter[date]` cannot widen it; it can only narrow it further.\n- **PAID** does real date-range querying: at most 3 years back, and any one `filter[date]` range may span at most a year. With no `filter[date]` at all you get everything from 3 years ago onwards, with no cap on future lessons.\n\nOn both plans, a lesson someone skipped and has not yet rebooked stays visible for 90 days regardless of the window, so nothing pending quietly falls off the end.\n\nWhen you filter by a single student with `filter[customerId]`, that student's own `attendance`, `attendanceUpdated`, `pendingMakeup` and `makeUpToActivityIds` are copied to the top level of each lesson, so you do not have to dig through `customers` to find them. That only happens for one student at a time, because attendance is inherently per-student.\n\n**Example queries**\n\n- `?filter[date][gte]=2026-03-16&filter[date][lte]=2026-03-22&sort=date,startTime`  \n  One week of the timetable, in the order you'd print it.\n- `?filter[customerId]=9101&filter[date][gte]=2026-03-01`  \n  One student's lessons from March onwards, with their attendance flattened onto each one.\n- `?filter[pendingMakeup]=true`  \n  Everyone who has skipped a lesson and still owes a make-up.\n- `?filter[attendance]=A&filter[date]=2026-03-17`  \n  Yesterday's absences, for a follow-up call list.\n- `?include=staffs,facilities&filter[date]=2026-03-17&fields[activity]=name,startTime,endTime`  \n  A room-and-teacher plan for one day, with nothing else in the payload.\n\n**Related records you can pull in** (`?include=`)\n\n- `center` — The centre the lesson belongs to, as `{id, name}`.\n- `customers` — Every student booked into the lesson, with their attendance, payment and make-up state. A booking's `status` is one of `W` weekly, `B` fortnightly, `T` single, `M` make-up, `S` skipped, `J` just-skipped, `N` normal, `Q` quit, `D` quit and moved up, `I` temporarily quit. _(already included by default — asking again changes nothing.)_\n- `staffs` — The teachers assigned to the lesson.\n- `facilities` — The rooms or resources booked for it.",
                "parameters": [
                    {
                        "name": "page",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Page number, starting at 1. How deep you may page is capped by your plan — 5 pages on FREE and 200 on PAID, independent of page size. Going past the cap is a `422`, not an empty page.",
                        "schema": {
                            "type": "integer",
                            "minimum": 1,
                            "default": 1
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "pageSize",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Records per page. Defaults to 50, with a hard ceiling of 500 and a plan ceiling of 50 on FREE / 500 on PAID. Asking for more than your plan allows returns `422` — it is never silently clamped.",
                        "schema": {
                            "type": "integer",
                            "minimum": 1,
                            "default": 50
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "perPage",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Alias for `pageSize`. If both are sent, `pageSize` wins.",
                        "schema": {
                            "type": "integer",
                            "minimum": 1
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "sort",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Sort by one or more fields, comma separated. Prefix a field with `-` to sort descending (`sort=-created,name`). Sorting by anything outside the list below is rejected.",
                        "schema": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "x-allowed-fields": [
                                "id",
                                "centerId",
                                "nextId",
                                "category",
                                "name",
                                "level",
                                "date",
                                "startDate",
                                "endDate",
                                "startTime",
                                "endTime",
                                "color",
                                "classId",
                                "number",
                                "quota",
                                "enrolled",
                                "detail",
                                "remark",
                                "moreInfo",
                                "created",
                                "updated",
                                "deleted"
                            ]
                        },
                        "example": "-created"
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "fields[activity]",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Return only these fields, comma separated. `id` is always included whether you ask for it or not. The key is the model's table name, so it is `fields[activity]` for this resource.",
                        "schema": {
                            "type": "string"
                        },
                        "example": "centerId,nextId,category"
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "include",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Related records to embed in the response, comma separated.",
                        "schema": {
                            "type": "string"
                        },
                        "example": "center,customers"
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "filter[attendance]",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Matches lessons where **any** booked student carries this attendance code. Combine with `filter[customerId]` to ask about one student. Exact match. A comma-separated list becomes an `IN (...)` match, and an explicit `null` matches rows where the field is unset. Operators: `eq`, `neq`, `ne`, `in`, `nin`, `exists` — e.g. `filter[attendance][eq]=…`, or the JSON form `filter[attendance]={\"eq\":\"…\"}`.",
                        "schema": {
                            "oneOf": [
                                {
                                    "type": "string"
                                },
                                {
                                    "type": "object",
                                    "additionalProperties": true,
                                    "x-operators": [
                                        "eq",
                                        "neq",
                                        "ne",
                                        "in",
                                        "nin",
                                        "exists"
                                    ]
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "filter[category]",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "The first level of the course name, copied onto the lesson when it is created. Partial match (`LIKE %value%`). A comma-separated list matches any of the values. Operators: `eq`, `neq`, `ne`, `in`, `nin`, `like`, `exists` — e.g. `filter[category][eq]=…`, or the JSON form `filter[category]={\"eq\":\"…\"}`.",
                        "schema": {
                            "oneOf": [
                                {
                                    "type": "string"
                                },
                                {
                                    "type": "object",
                                    "additionalProperties": true,
                                    "x-operators": [
                                        "eq",
                                        "neq",
                                        "ne",
                                        "in",
                                        "nin",
                                        "like",
                                        "exists"
                                    ]
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "filter[centerId]",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Exact match. A comma-separated list becomes an `IN (...)` match, and an explicit `null` matches rows where the field is unset. Operators: `eq`, `neq`, `ne`, `in`, `nin`, `exists` — e.g. `filter[centerId][eq]=…`, or the JSON form `filter[centerId]={\"eq\":\"…\"}`.",
                        "schema": {
                            "oneOf": [
                                {
                                    "type": "string"
                                },
                                {
                                    "type": "object",
                                    "additionalProperties": true,
                                    "x-operators": [
                                        "eq",
                                        "neq",
                                        "ne",
                                        "in",
                                        "nin",
                                        "exists"
                                    ]
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "filter[classId]",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Groups a repeating series together. Every lesson generated from the same schedule shares it. Exact match. A comma-separated list becomes an `IN (...)` match, and an explicit `null` matches rows where the field is unset. Operators: `eq`, `neq`, `ne`, `in`, `nin`, `exists` — e.g. `filter[classId][eq]=…`, or the JSON form `filter[classId]={\"eq\":\"…\"}`.",
                        "schema": {
                            "oneOf": [
                                {
                                    "type": "string"
                                },
                                {
                                    "type": "object",
                                    "additionalProperties": true,
                                    "x-operators": [
                                        "eq",
                                        "neq",
                                        "ne",
                                        "in",
                                        "nin",
                                        "exists"
                                    ]
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "name": "filter[color]",
                        "in": "query",
                        "description": "Partial match (`LIKE %value%`). A comma-separated list matches any of the values. Operators: `eq`, `neq`, `ne`, `in`, `nin`, `like`, `exists` — e.g. `filter[color][eq]=…`, or the JSON form `filter[color]={\"eq\":\"…\"}`.",
                        "schema": {
                            "oneOf": [
                                {
                                    "type": "string"
                                },
                                {
                                    "type": "object",
                                    "additionalProperties": true,
                                    "x-operators": [
                                        "eq",
                                        "neq",
                                        "ne",
                                        "in",
                                        "nin",
